WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.860
Facebook is an idealistic and optimistic company.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:19.090
For most of our existence, we focused on all
the good that connecting people can do.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:26.170
Mr. Zuckerberg, what is Facebook doing to
prevent foreign actors from interfering in

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:27.720
US elections?

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:33.309
If you've messaged anybody this week, would
you share with us the names of the people

00:00:33.309 --> 00:00:34.340
you've messaged?

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:38.510
Senator, no, I would probably not choose to
do that publicly here.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.900
I think that might be what this is all about.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.370
Do you believe you're more responsible with
millions of Americans' personal data than

00:00:45.370 --> 00:01:01.920
the Federal Government would be?

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.129
The early internet is a really interesting
and fascinating to study.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:10.640
It was a time when pretty much everyone who
was involved was really optimistic about what

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.090
it could mean.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:18.040
The web is going to be the defining social
moment for computing.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.580
It will give free access to knowledge to people
when you really needed it.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:27.360
It suddenly seemed to shrink the world, and
suddenly even being a part of vast distances

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.570
from people didn't matter as much.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:34.530
Move fast and break things, kids coming out
of dorm rooms and starting companies.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:37.940
All the walls that used to separate people
from information and from each other were

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:42.140
being broken down and used to create again
a new open egalitarian world.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:47.709
American society and American political culture
allowed these companies to grow and to mediate

00:01:47.709 --> 00:01:50.720
all our interactions now that we have.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.820
People were thinking like is technology good
for us?

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.840
Is the Internet good for us?

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.560
This will change everything.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:05.330
I think it was clear that the main goal was
to get it to a stable version and monetize

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:06.330
it.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:12.640
Business is working well, the majority of
our revenues, of course, are in advertising.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.040
No one really knew how fast things would evolve.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:28.810
No one really knew how quickly and basically
how willingly platforms were willing to sell

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:32.590
out users.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:36.860
When I first started covering Silicon Valley,
I began to try to understand how Silicon Valley

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:39.090
companies like Facebook and Google made money.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:41.060
What was their business model?

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:45.719
The earliest moment that I can remember when
privacy became an issue, the Internet experience,

00:02:45.719 --> 00:02:48.720
was in 2004 when Google launched Gmail.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.109
It was going to do that, it was going to finance
this by essentially reading all your emails

00:02:53.109 --> 00:02:54.549
and then serving you targeted advertising.

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:58.840
This was a new thing for most people because
people didn't realize how Google was financing

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.780
its operations until then.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:06.050
Suddenly people became much more savvy about
what was happening on the Internet.

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:10.810
A decade ago, like 15 years ago, I would not
have found this was possible, not just in

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:14.530
terms of ethically, but also technology-wise.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:19.859
Changing your privacy settings at this point
is like rearranging the chairs on the Titanic

00:03:19.859 --> 00:03:21.620
in a way.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:25.849
Not only because already a lot of this data
has been collected and curated, but also because

00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:34.340
you even when you change your settings, there
are ways for people to still scrape information

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:38.090
from these websites.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:44.349
The thing that is interesting to me the most
currently is the result that algorithms play

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:48.810
in the formation of these online communities.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:55.769
The way these algorithms work is they find
patterns in data by looking at what humans

00:03:55.769 --> 00:03:59.560
have provided them as examples.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:04.530
This is one area where buyers could actually
be introduced into algorithms.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:11.370
If you think of a word like CEO and a word
like man, most likely what you'll see if you

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:16.340
mapped him into this vector space, you have
man here and most likely a CEO is going to

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:19.950
be very close to man, but very far away from
woman.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:23.660
Looking at this type of algorithm, you could
also envision how a system like Google would

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:27.420
start to use these type of results.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:31.160
People are really pushing forward for its
algorithms to be used in every facet of human

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.160
life.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:38.540
We don't actually understand fully the consequences.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:43.960
Social media has the power to bring people
together formally like without a voice.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:47.240
We've seen hopeful stories, like the Arab
Spring.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:52.350
We've seen the MeToo Movement, we've seen
Black Lives Matter.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.690
The Internet is a great organizing tool.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:59.560
Are we better off now after the Internet has
come into existence than we were 30 years

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:00.560
ago?

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:01.630
Well, it's changed it.

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:08.700
It's made some things much better, but again
influence is how it makes money.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:13.650
Platforms generally need to acknowledge that
they have responsibility.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:19.600
Users should have a reasonable amount of trust
in a platform, in a service they're using

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.000
that their data is not being sold.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:26.560
Some of these companies have started to realize
the role that they've played and are moving

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.710
towards solutions to that end.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:35.950
Here's the question that all of us got to
answer, given what's happened here, why we

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:37.200
should let you self-regulate?

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:42.150
Well, Senator, my position is not that there
should be no regulation.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:43.691
I think the Internet is increasingly important.

00:05:43.691 --> 00:05:45.200
Do you embrace regulation?

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:51.300
So, we're going to begin our program with
one of the forefathers of virtual reality.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.660
He was there in the beginning since the early
1980s, I believe.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:59.680
He helped craft a vision for the Internet
as a way to bring people together.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:05.500
He's a scientist, a musician, and an author,
and his new book is Ten Arguments for Deleting

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:08.480
Your Social Media Accounts right now.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.880
Please welcome Jaron Lanier.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:13.880
Jaron, good to see you.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:15.080
Hello, hello.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:21.810
Well, you've said that we don't have to throw
the whole thing away, but you also say that

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:27.640
this Internet AI has turned us into Pavlovian
dogs carrying around devices suitable for

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.840
what you call mass behavior modification.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:32.700
What do you mean by that?

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:40.190
Ah, well, there was a time when we used the
mail, and I should point out when you sent

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:42.730
a letter by mail, nobody else read it.

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:44.900
You paid for it, but it was strictly your
business.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:49.850
It was a remarkable thing, almost unimaginable
today.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:56.550
So in those days, you could find yourself
under observation by somebody who's trying

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.450
to be sneaky and manipulate you.

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:03.020
One way to do it would be to volunteer for
a psychology test in the basement of the psych

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:09.190
building on campus, and then some undergraduates
would be behind the one-way mirror trying

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:14.020
to get you to do something, or you could get
into an abusive relationship, or you could

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:16.580
join a cult.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:20.170
You could find yourself on the wrong side
of an interrogation desk.

00:07:20.170 --> 00:07:27.030
There are all kinds of ways it could happen,
but they were all very localized and unusual.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:29.770
Now it's happening to everybody.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:36.050
Everybody is under constant observation and
constant manipulation, albeit just to be very

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:38.750
clear, it's slight.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:46.280
So at this point, the degree of observation
is less than in the scenarios I just described,

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:51.210
although it's getting more so every day.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:57.300
The thing is, slight differences, slight behavior
modification applied consistently over time

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:58.300
can shift society.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:07:59.700
It's like compound interest.

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:03.790
At any particular moment, you might say, oh,
how much difference could it make?

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:07.440
So I saw an ad from the Russian intelligence
warfare people.

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:11.010
So it got me a little cranky over something
or other, who cares?

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:15.180
But cumulatively, it does have a statistical
effect on the whole society.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:18.290
Then as far as why I'm asking people to quit,
I'll tell you why.

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:20.470
It's 'cause I love Silicon Valley.

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:22.010
I love making digital products.

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:23.110
I've sold a company to Google.

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:24.300
I'm working with Microsoft.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:25.940
I adore my community.

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:30.750
It's my home, and I don't want you to be passive
sheep customers.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:35.479
I want you to be demanding tough customers,
and I want you to make us work to make you

00:08:35.479 --> 00:08:36.479
happy.

00:08:36.479 --> 00:08:39.300
Right now, you're all turning into passive
sheep, and it bugs me.

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:40.300
All right.

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:42.529
So if you quit, you're at least prodding us.

00:08:42.529 --> 00:08:46.449
You're at least not just sitting there passively
accepting whatever we feed you.

00:08:46.449 --> 00:08:47.790
So I think it would be better for us.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:53.209
I want you to challenge us, because that way
we're not disconnected from the world anymore.

00:08:53.209 --> 00:08:54.800
Can you see that?

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:59.050
Like when you engage with Silicon Valley,
when you're tough, you're actually saving

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:05.430
us from being abandoned on some desolate island
of the super empowered.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:06.949
You actually help us.

00:09:06.949 --> 00:09:09.050
You actually reconnect the world.

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:15.069
To get precise about what you're saying, the
trend lines are being watched, in how we do

00:09:15.069 --> 00:09:19.990
respond in tiny bits, are being watched by
algorithms that can see what the trend is

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:22.680
pointing to, and so it subtlety changes our
behavior.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:23.680
Is that how it works?

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:30.529
Yeah, the way it works is there are very few
sort of sneaky people in cubicles trying to

00:09:30.529 --> 00:09:34.360
figure out how to manipulate you directly.

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:38.009
The Russians have some in their employee,
but the much more common thing that makes

00:09:38.009 --> 00:09:40.050
the whole system work is algorithms.

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:43.420
So the algorithm will do a little bit of random
stuff.

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:49.519
It will say, well, during what time of the
month is this person more susceptible to this

00:09:49.519 --> 00:09:50.890
kind of pitch?

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:53.430
What about other people who correlate with
this person in some way?

00:09:53.430 --> 00:10:00.250
So all of these correlations turn into this
statistical, I wouldn't call it a model of

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:05.670
you because it wouldn't pass muster as a scientific
model, but it's kind of a predictive portrait

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:09.639
of you that can be used slightly reliably.

00:10:09.639 --> 00:10:14.339
I mean this stuff is only barely better than
random, but it is a little better than random.

00:10:14.339 --> 00:10:18.100
So it gradually finds a way to engage with
you.

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:19.850
We use the word engage instead of addict.

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:21.589
What I mean is addict.

00:10:21.589 --> 00:10:26.800
We gradually addict you through algorithmic
exploration until we find whatever it is that

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:28.040
will get you.

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:30.959
What's the business model behind all of that?

00:10:30.959 --> 00:10:36.950
Well, the business model is it's really kind
of interesting.

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:42.790
I've had occasion to be at these events where
all of the Silicon Valley companies sell their

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:45.899
biggest advertisers big package deals for
the next year.

00:10:45.899 --> 00:10:49.670
It's an amazing spectacle to behold, because
each of the companies in turn, Facebook and

00:10:49.670 --> 00:10:54.310
Google and so on, will grab a stage and then
present to the biggest advertising buyers,

00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:57.180
often I kid you not with dancers and special
effects.

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:58.990
It's like this big production.

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:03.000
If you listen to the way the companies talk
about what they can do to their own customers,

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:08.459
the advertisers, you hear this extraordinary
bragging, like "We can really nail in on a

00:11:08.459 --> 00:11:09.459
person.

00:11:09.459 --> 00:11:10.459
We can get them to do something."

00:11:10.459 --> 00:11:12.170
It's actually a little exaggerated.

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:14.790
Then the public face is, "Oh, no, no, no,
no.

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:16.139
We only do things in your interest."

00:11:16.139 --> 00:11:17.930
But it really is kind of two-faced.

00:11:17.930 --> 00:11:22.399
There's this very aggressive way that the
companies sell themselves.

00:11:22.399 --> 00:11:26.259
I think what happens to the advertisers, to
the people who are putting all these many

00:11:26.259 --> 00:11:30.550
billions of dollars into the system, is they
get scared because they're thinking, "Oh my

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:33.910
god, if we don't pay into these companies,
it's like we won't exist."

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:39.500
It's kind of like an existential version of
a mafia protection racket.

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:44.199
It's like you're saying, "You pay us or nobody
will know about you."

00:11:44.199 --> 00:11:49.589
I think while the official version is that
we're giving you the ads that are more useful,

00:11:49.589 --> 00:11:54.060
and then the sales version is we're able to
mind control people for your benefit, the

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:57.829
actual truth is we're going to scare you into
thinking if you don't give us money, you'll

00:11:57.829 --> 00:11:58.829
cease to exist.

00:11:58.829 --> 00:12:01.899
So I think those are the three different versions
of the same business plan.

00:12:01.899 --> 00:12:07.180
And if I'm not mistaken, you've actually written
somewhere that all of this is turning into,

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:12.399
these are your words, quote, you said, "into
assholes."

00:12:12.399 --> 00:12:16.999
Right, well, I wanted to sound presidential.

00:12:16.999 --> 00:12:25.249
I should say something about that.

00:12:25.249 --> 00:12:26.730
Oh, I see.

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:30.920
What happens is that when you get somebody
addicted to something, whether it's an opioid

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:33.360
or social media, their personality changes.

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:35.230
They get kind of cranky and selfish.

00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:36.230
There's this thing.

00:12:36.230 --> 00:12:41.129
Anybody who's known an addict will recognize
this, and social media addicts do tend to

00:12:41.129 --> 00:12:46.449
get this quality, which is sometimes called
the snowflake personality where it's almost

00:12:46.449 --> 00:12:48.600
like they're asking for a fight.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:50.519
They want attention so badly.

00:12:50.519 --> 00:12:54.060
So it does kind of make you into that thing
that was mentioned.

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:58.240
I've heard, you've pointed out that some of
the tech titans, of which there are not that

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:00.769
many, don't let their kids go anywhere near
technology.

00:13:00.769 --> 00:13:03.370
of my friends in the industry are shocked.

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:05.129
I kind of let my daughter do whatever she
wants.

00:13:05.129 --> 00:13:07.119
I figure she has to learn her own lessons,
and they're shocked.

00:13:07.119 --> 00:13:08.600
"You let her have a phone?"

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:11.269
I'm like, well, she's going to have to figure
it out.

00:13:11.269 --> 00:13:16.079
But a lot of people are incredibly anti-tech
within the tech community.

00:13:16.079 --> 00:13:17.079
It's rather remarkable.

00:13:17.079 --> 00:13:22.559
And you've also said that this is approaching
something like the divine right of kings,

00:13:22.559 --> 00:13:29.240
turning us into people who are following without
realizing that we're following, but we're

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:31.060
just giving over to it anyway?

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:34.420
It is the strangest thing.

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:40.110
I don't have anything against the individuals
I'll mention 'cause a lot of them are people

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.480
I know, but it's really weird.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:47.269
One of the tech companies is the first large
public company controlled by a single person

00:13:47.269 --> 00:13:49.399
and has all of this power.

00:13:49.399 --> 00:13:51.230
Why should that be so?

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:57.389
It's a very peculiar thing, but we had such
a fervent belief in the myth of the hacker

00:13:57.389 --> 00:14:05.149
cowboy who would dent reality, in the words
of Steve Jobs, that somehow we're enacting

00:14:05.149 --> 00:14:06.149
it.

00:14:06.149 --> 00:14:10.920
One of the things I'm a little concerned of
actually is that sometimes when a society

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:16.069
chooses figures to be godlike figures, then
they'll tear them down.

00:14:16.069 --> 00:14:20.790
So they'll elect a Mussolini and then hang
the Mussolini, or they'll elect the Aztec

00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:25.519
prince for a year, and then sacrifice that
person.

00:14:25.519 --> 00:14:32.179
I'm a little concerned about some kind of
wave of hatred against tech, which would be

00:14:32.179 --> 00:14:36.430
totally inappropriate because we actually
are doing something important.

00:14:36.430 --> 00:14:41.029
What I ask is not to hate us, but to engage
us and to force us to be better.

00:14:41.029 --> 00:14:42.269
That's a much better scenario.

00:14:42.269 --> 00:14:46.930
I can understand that, and yet we've seen
examples lately where it's being pointed out

00:14:46.930 --> 00:14:55.309
to us that the smart phones conveying what
they're able to convey are turning into propagators

00:14:55.309 --> 00:15:00.339
of maniacal social violence, weaponized in
Myanmar, the Rohingya problem.

00:15:00.339 --> 00:15:04.119
In South Sudan, social media is literally
a deadly weapon.

00:15:04.119 --> 00:15:06.059
In South Sudan, explain that.

00:15:06.059 --> 00:15:10.430
Yeah, so this has been one of the awful things
that's happened.

00:15:10.430 --> 00:15:18.100
Early on, Silicon Valley is part of the Bay
Area in California and tends to swing left,

00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:19.100
right?

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:25.869
Very early on, there was this, I would say,
a certainty that if you just let people talk

00:15:25.869 --> 00:15:31.420
to each other, it'll create positive leftist
changes in the world.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:34.809
One of the first great triumphs of that was
the Arab Spring, like, oh, we'll just give

00:15:34.809 --> 00:15:35.839
power to the people.

00:15:35.839 --> 00:15:36.899
They'll overthrow dictators.

00:15:36.899 --> 00:15:39.050
They'll create this beautiful democracy.

00:15:39.050 --> 00:15:45.600
Now, the thing is the algorithms that are
watching participants in something like the

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:47.739
Arab Spring aren't left or right.

00:15:47.739 --> 00:15:49.239
They're not humanistic.

00:15:49.239 --> 00:15:51.269
They have no feelings at all.

00:15:51.269 --> 00:15:57.170
They're just trying to find any path to maximize
addiction and maximize influence algorithmically,

00:15:57.170 --> 00:15:58.690
just through searching.

00:15:58.690 --> 00:16:04.779
Eventually, they discover, the algorithms
discover in a blind algorithmic way that the

00:16:04.779 --> 00:16:07.720
negative emotions, the negative people are
easier to engage.

00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:11.029
So everything starts tilting towards amplifying
those people.

00:16:11.029 --> 00:16:15.759
So you'll have an ISIS that gets even more
mileage from the same tools that propelled

00:16:15.759 --> 00:16:21.240
an Arab Spring, or you'll get a resurgent
KKK and neo-Nazi movie that gets even more

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:27.279
mileage from the same nexus of communications
that propelled the Black Lives Matter, is

00:16:27.279 --> 00:16:29.649
an example that happened here.

00:16:29.649 --> 00:16:32.309
We see this again and again.

00:16:32.309 --> 00:16:34.410
Can I get just slightly geeky for a second?

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:35.410
Please, please.

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:36.410
Okay.

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:38.350
But you geeks have given this to us.

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:45.569
Yeah, okay, well, then thank you for giving
us another chance to screw you up here.

00:16:45.569 --> 00:16:51.790
In classical behaviorism, the earliest experiments
used things like candy and electric shocks

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:55.739
to give people stimulus response to what they
did to change their behavior, right?

00:16:55.739 --> 00:16:57.850
That's the Skinner box.

00:16:57.850 --> 00:17:03.529
Now, we don't yet have drones hovering over
people dropping candy or electric shocks on

00:17:03.529 --> 00:17:04.529
them, right?

00:17:04.529 --> 00:17:10.780
That's coming, but it's not here yet, so we
use symbols, analogous to Pavlov's bell.

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:15.939
But we use social experience to give people
little dopamine hits, as we call them in the

00:17:15.939 --> 00:17:20.209
trade, the little positive feeling when you
get retweeted or something, and then the negative

00:17:20.209 --> 00:17:29.830
ones, when you get treated badly, when you
get harassed online, insulted.

00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:38.690
So the thing is that in a broad sense, positive
and negative stimuli are both powerful in

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:40.919
people, but they have different time profiles.

00:17:40.919 --> 00:17:46.000
So what happens is the positive ones can take
longer to build and can be broken more quickly,

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:47.809
and the negative ones can come up faster.

00:17:47.809 --> 00:17:53.910
So it's faster to get startled or scared than
it is to relax, but it takes longer to build

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:55.540
trust than it takes to lose trust.

00:17:55.540 --> 00:17:57.350
So you can see, they're reverses.

00:17:57.350 --> 00:18:02.049
So since the algorithms are responding on
a rapid feedback loop, they select out and

00:18:02.049 --> 00:18:06.820
amplify the negative stuff, and that's why
ISIS gets more mileage than the Arab Spring,

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:09.360
and that's why the Klan gets more mileage
than Black Lives Matter.

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:10.360
So what's the solution?

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:15.320
Do we have to step away and abandon the free
model?

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:17.380
think there's a variety of possible solutions.

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:21.419
The one that I'm really interested in is changing
the underlying business model.

00:18:21.419 --> 00:18:27.390
So if you have an underlying business model
where the only way you can make a penny, what

00:18:27.390 --> 00:18:28.580
we're doing now is so insane.

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:32.230
Can you imagine if in the old days, if you
mailed somebody a letter, instead of buying

00:18:32.230 --> 00:18:38.880
postage, the letter would be free, but some
customer would only pay money if they were

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:42.780
confident that they could change the letter
in a way that they would influence events.

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:43.950
That would be extraordinary, right?

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:45.159
But that's exactly what we've done.

00:18:45.159 --> 00:18:48.390
Right now, if you and I are to have contact
over the Internet, this thing that's supposed

00:18:48.390 --> 00:18:52.840
to be open and free, the only way that can
be financed is by a third party who we don't

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:55.500
know about who wishes to influence us both.

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:59.549
So once you have that business model, it's
like this red carpet invitation to the Russians

00:18:59.549 --> 00:19:04.899
and other bad actors in the Klan and whoever
to use it, because they can get incredible

00:19:04.899 --> 00:19:08.790
mileage out of it of a disruptive and negative
sort.

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:11.340
So bring back postage.

00:19:11.340 --> 00:19:15.120
Make it paid.

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:19.390
In the days when Facebook was being founded,
everybody thought that the way movies and

00:19:19.390 --> 00:19:22.110
TV would be created would be like the Wikipedia.

00:19:22.110 --> 00:19:24.330
It would be this massive volunteer free thing.

00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:26.880
Everybody in Silicon Valley, not in Hollywood.

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:28.410
We had an honest test.

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:29.410
That was tried.

00:19:29.410 --> 00:19:33.399
A lot of people tried to do that, but then
we had Netflix, HBO, Hulu, et cetera, and

00:19:33.399 --> 00:19:37.809
it turns out that when people pay for TV,
we got this thing called peak TV.

00:19:37.809 --> 00:19:40.710
We did an honest test, we got an honest result.

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:45.610
So what I want Facebook to turn into, I don't
want the good stuff on Facebook to disappear,

00:19:45.610 --> 00:19:50.230
I think there's a lot of extremely positive
stuff that happens on social media, I want

00:19:50.230 --> 00:19:54.850
it to turn into a cross between Netflix and
Etsy.

00:19:54.850 --> 00:19:58.649
I want you to pay for it like you pay for
Netflix so you can get peak social media,

00:19:58.649 --> 00:20:01.750
but if you do something special that means
a lot, I want you to be able to get money

00:20:01.750 --> 00:20:03.440
for your craft, like you would on Etsy.

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:05.580
So I want that to be the future of Facebook.

00:20:05.580 --> 00:20:08.770
It sounds good, but to get a little bit realistic,
that would take an awful lot.

00:20:08.770 --> 00:20:11.170
Do you think any technology company would
actually go for this?

00:20:11.170 --> 00:20:12.170
They'll do better.

00:20:12.170 --> 00:20:15.919
I mean, this is just normal capitalism.

00:20:15.919 --> 00:20:20.441
This idea of people coming in from the side
and getting in the middle of everybody and

00:20:20.441 --> 00:20:23.539
manipulating society as the only business
model is not capitalism.

00:20:23.539 --> 00:20:27.350
It's some bizarre dystopic thing.

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:31.360
I'm suggesting just normal capitalism, like
people pay for stuff they like and they have

00:20:31.360 --> 00:20:32.360
a choice about it.

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:35.470
It's just normal.

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:38.790
I think it would be better for Facebook, it
would be better for Google, I think it would

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:39.790
be better for everybody.

00:20:39.790 --> 00:20:41.340
I think their shareholders would be happy.

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:43.460
I think it's just the better solution.

00:20:43.460 --> 00:20:45.490
If we could figure out how to have that happen
all at once.

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:46.580
But let me ask you one other question.

00:20:46.580 --> 00:20:47.580
All right.

00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:51.539
You've said that this was all making politics
impossible as we understand it.

00:20:51.539 --> 00:20:59.039
I must say because the way the rights of individuals
for voting and all was conceived in an age

00:20:59.039 --> 00:21:05.480
when we didn't have any of this magical stuff,
it's changed the definition of politics almost.

00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:10.880
Yeah, I mean we live in a bizarre world now
that just happened recently.

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:15.490
I feel sorry for young people who couldn't
experience the contrast with what came earlier

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:23.020
where nothing seems real, everything seems
like it's manipulated by unseen forces, everybody's

00:21:23.020 --> 00:21:25.120
on edge all the time.

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:32.190
Not that we had utopia before, but we had
a little bit more reality.

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:33.880
We must get back there to survive.

00:21:33.880 --> 00:21:36.919
I don't see how we can survive if we're insane.

00:21:36.919 --> 00:21:40.190
But one big question is whether anyone's criticism
will matter?

00:21:40.190 --> 00:21:43.899
So there's this bit of a confusion on this
matter.

00:21:43.899 --> 00:21:47.480
Sometime in the Silicon Valley atmosphere,
there's this thing like, well, if you're optimistic,

00:21:47.480 --> 00:21:51.390
it means your complacent 'cause you're sure
things will work out automatically, it'll

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:52.580
just naturally get better.

00:21:52.580 --> 00:21:54.250
I don't think that's ever been true.

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:58.570
I think we have progressed and things have
gotten better and the various trend lines

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:02.830
of betterment are very real, but every single
increment of that betterment was due to somebody

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:05.059
putting their foot down and saying things
can be better.

00:22:05.059 --> 00:22:09.809
Jaron Lanier, thank you very much for joining
us and ending on a positive note.

00:22:09.809 --> 00:22:12.289
Thank you very much.

00:22:12.289 --> 00:22:14.769
Thank you so much.

00:22:14.769 --> 00:22:19.600
What is the science behind these AI systems
that are impacting everything from the news

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:25.210
you read, healthcare, who gets hired, promoted,
deported, What does living in a world run

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:26.559
by algorithms mean?

00:22:26.559 --> 00:22:32.150
Joining us now to dig deeper into all of this,
Director of the Harvard and MIT Ethics and

00:22:32.150 --> 00:22:40.289
Governance AI Initiative and the former Google
Global Public Policy lead for artificial intelligence

00:22:40.289 --> 00:22:45.659
and machine learning, please welcome Tim Hwang.

00:22:45.659 --> 00:22:47.139
Tim?

00:22:47.139 --> 00:22:54.059
Co-founder of the AI Now Institute that's
based at NYU, she is a research scientist

00:22:54.059 --> 00:22:57.419
and founder of the Google Open Research Group.

00:22:57.419 --> 00:23:00.799
Please welcome Meredith Whittaker.

00:23:00.799 --> 00:23:09.950
Aviv Ovadya is Chief Technologist at the Center
for Social Media Responsibility at the University

00:23:09.950 --> 00:23:11.890
of Michigan School of Information.

00:23:11.890 --> 00:23:19.460
He has worked to improve and provided research
for Amazon, the MIT Media Lab, for Morgan

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:25.720
Stanley, Quora, Yerdle, the Tow Center for
Digital Journalism, and Google.

00:23:25.720 --> 00:23:29.489
Please welcome Aviv Ovadya.

00:23:29.489 --> 00:23:35.340
And last but not least, a professor of law,
business, and economics at Villanova University,

00:23:35.340 --> 00:23:42.320
he's also an affiliated scholar of the Center
for Internet Society at Stanford Law School.

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:47.279
Please welcome Brett Frischmann.

00:23:47.279 --> 00:23:49.720
So let's start with Brett.

00:23:49.720 --> 00:23:56.240
Your book, Re-Engineering Humanity, posits
that technology is turning us into machines.

00:23:56.240 --> 00:23:57.750
Is it?

00:23:57.750 --> 00:24:04.770
Well, the idea behind the book is to get us
to ask ourselves how often in our life you

00:24:04.770 --> 00:24:11.020
feel like or you behave as if automatically
or comparable to a machine, right?

00:24:11.020 --> 00:24:15.980
So the idea of thinking about, am I performing
a script?

00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:21.830
Am I behaving habitually or automatically,
and if so who wrote that script?

00:24:21.830 --> 00:24:27.770
So the idea is to think more and more about
how the technical systems, the world we're

00:24:27.770 --> 00:24:34.260
building or really engineering for ourselves
affects who we are as human beings, how we

00:24:34.260 --> 00:24:38.490
think, how we relate to each other, how we
interact with each other in different settings.

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:41.470
Tim, let me ask you about the good and bad.

00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:44.590
AI seems to be a label that's put on everything
these days.

00:24:44.590 --> 00:24:46.610
It sure is.

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:51.019
What does the term AI really mean in the context
that we have here?

00:24:51.019 --> 00:24:54.240
Training computers to perform tasks, how do
you train a computer?

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:55.240
That's right.

00:24:55.240 --> 00:24:58.519
So, when you talk about AI, it's important
to keep two things separate.

00:24:58.519 --> 00:25:03.110
One of them is the marketing of AI, which
is what you're referring to, and then there's

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:06.149
the sort of computer science of AI.

00:25:06.149 --> 00:25:11.360
Really that is a subfield of artificial intelligence
that's known as machine learning.

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:15.519
The basics of machine learning that you need
to know is it's sort of the study of algorithms

00:25:15.519 --> 00:25:18.230
that get better the more data that they see.

00:25:18.230 --> 00:25:22.559
So the notion is if you want to teach a machine
to understand how to recognize a cat in an

00:25:22.559 --> 00:25:25.460
image, you show it lots of images of cats.

00:25:25.460 --> 00:25:30.539
There's a certain set of methodologies that
are used to allow it to accomplish that task,

00:25:30.539 --> 00:25:34.100
and really that's the subfield that's been
driving really a lot of the progress that

00:25:34.100 --> 00:25:35.799
you've seen in the last few years.

00:25:35.799 --> 00:25:38.169
These machines are learning machines.

00:25:38.169 --> 00:25:40.760
How does that really work?

00:25:40.760 --> 00:25:45.299
So, a lot of it is based on the recognition
of what's known as features.

00:25:45.299 --> 00:25:51.390
For example, if you imagined trying to recognize
a cat, the example that I just gave you earlier,

00:25:51.390 --> 00:25:54.139
back in the day the idea is that you get a
bunch of programs together to think about

00:25:54.139 --> 00:25:58.669
how they recognize a cat themselves, and then
they'd program explicit rules into a machine.

00:25:58.669 --> 00:26:02.779
So they'd say, "Well, a cat's fuzzy and a
cat is usually these kinds of colors."

00:26:02.779 --> 00:26:04.679
You'd actually put those rules in.

00:26:04.679 --> 00:26:08.669
One of the unique things about machine learning
though is that it's able to identify these

00:26:08.669 --> 00:26:11.110
features by itself.

00:26:11.110 --> 00:26:15.169
After lots and lots of examples, machines
can do what they call inference.

00:26:15.169 --> 00:26:18.950
They're able to say, "Well, in all the cases
that I've seen, these are the types of things

00:26:18.950 --> 00:26:19.950
that are associated with cats."

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:23.340
It turns out that once you do it that way,
you get these systems that are much, much

00:26:23.340 --> 00:26:27.419
better than we had before in doing these types
of tasks.

00:26:27.419 --> 00:26:28.840
Better, smarter?

00:26:28.840 --> 00:26:30.690
Sure, depending on how you define smart.

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:33.679
That's part of our problem here today.

00:26:33.679 --> 00:26:36.850
So, Meredith,
You've said that we've rushed these technologies

00:26:36.850 --> 00:26:39.980
into some of the most sensitive areas of our
lives,

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:46.789
I'm going to ask your permission to go off
script immediately and sort of answer the

00:26:46.789 --> 00:26:51.179
question you asked to Tim, 'cause I think
we need to set up where we are right now socially

00:26:51.179 --> 00:26:52.570
and politically.

00:26:52.570 --> 00:26:53.570
Fine.

00:26:53.570 --> 00:26:55.300
Then we can get into some of the examples.

00:26:55.300 --> 00:27:03.399
But when you ask what AI is, I have been in
the tech industry for almost 12 years now.

00:27:03.399 --> 00:27:06.740
I have thought a lot about these issues.

00:27:06.740 --> 00:27:12.149
That's not an easy question to answer, even
for somebody who is enmeshed in these technologies.

00:27:12.149 --> 00:27:21.409
That's partly because while it's an old field,
over 60 years old, it is newly adopted in

00:27:21.409 --> 00:27:24.790
the last five to ten years, and everyone can
attest to this.

00:27:24.790 --> 00:27:29.720
You can't pass a newsstand without seeing
another shiny white robot and some promise

00:27:29.720 --> 00:27:32.509
of singularity futures, right?

00:27:32.509 --> 00:27:35.779
I think we have to ask, okay, why is this?

00:27:35.779 --> 00:27:40.169
Why did this just crop up like mushrooms everywhere
we go?

00:27:40.169 --> 00:27:44.620
When you ask that question, you begin to see
that, oh, this happened right around the time

00:27:44.620 --> 00:27:51.429
that big tech corporations were consolidating,
that this business model had taken over.

00:27:51.429 --> 00:27:57.169
Where finally you have these organizations
that have incredible compute power, they have

00:27:57.169 --> 00:27:58.659
super computing infrastructure.

00:27:58.659 --> 00:28:03.790
They are actually able to process the massive
amounts of data that is needed to, as Tim

00:28:03.790 --> 00:28:07.559
said, train these systems to recognize patterns.

00:28:07.559 --> 00:28:14.740
They have this data because they have vast
market reach, from Facebook to Google to whatever

00:28:14.740 --> 00:28:19.130
other app you've installed, they are able
to continually collect huge amounts of data.

00:28:19.130 --> 00:28:25.370
They have the infrastructure to store it,
and then they have incredibly talented engineers

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:28.840
that they can afford to pay to build these
algorithms.

00:28:28.840 --> 00:28:39.491
So we look at this as sort of a commodification
of a earlier theoretical, I would say, not

00:28:39.491 --> 00:28:44.080
a prominent branch of computer science for
many years that suddenly became marketable.

00:28:44.080 --> 00:28:48.020
So we have to ask when we look at AI, whose
stories are we hearing?

00:28:48.020 --> 00:28:52.090
And a lot of times, what we're hearing are
the stories that are written by the marketing

00:28:52.090 --> 00:28:56.820
departments of the corporations that have
recognized the marketability and the profitability

00:28:56.820 --> 00:29:00.820
of these techniques that have been around
for a while.

00:29:00.820 --> 00:29:02.350
That raises the question of motive.

00:29:02.350 --> 00:29:07.620
For example, you have written and spoken about
the fact that there are algorithms that claim

00:29:07.620 --> 00:29:12.830
to be able to discover which people are more
likely to commit a crime.

00:29:12.830 --> 00:29:20.360
Absolutely, and you have to ask, if you look
at one of the companies that has claimed to

00:29:20.360 --> 00:29:25.929
be able to do this and their researchers and
businesses that are looking at this as a technology

00:29:25.929 --> 00:29:33.040
is Axon, which used to be called Taser, which
has a huge cache of police body cam data.

00:29:33.040 --> 00:29:39.000
So they acquired two AI companies about a
year and a half ago and are busy creating

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:45.010
real time facial recognition systems that
will analyze the data captured by police body

00:29:45.010 --> 00:29:46.010
cams.

00:29:46.010 --> 00:29:49.860
Now their premise is, hey, police encounter
criminals, we have this dataset that will

00:29:49.860 --> 00:29:51.850
then model criminality.

00:29:51.850 --> 00:29:56.830
We'll train the AI to see what a criminal
looks like, if you're thinking eugenics, physiognomy,

00:29:56.830 --> 00:29:59.210
phrenology, you're right.

00:29:59.210 --> 00:30:01.950
Then we will be able to identify pre-crime.

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:05.740
Innocent until proven guilty, it sounds like
it's under some kind of threat.

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:06.740
Yeah.

00:30:06.740 --> 00:30:10.600
Yeah, and I think that's part of the concern
around the marketing of it, because I think

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:14.509
there is such a big feeding frenzy over the
promise of the technology.

00:30:14.509 --> 00:30:17.250
That's often quite at odds with what the technology
can actually do.

00:30:17.250 --> 00:30:20.780
So I think part of the concern is that you
actually see this technology being deployed

00:30:20.780 --> 00:30:25.669
in all sorts of situations where it's not
ready for primetime and may not ever be ready

00:30:25.669 --> 00:30:26.669
for primetime.

00:30:26.669 --> 00:30:29.879
I think we have to ask the categorical question
of even if it worked as well as we believe

00:30:29.879 --> 00:30:32.760
it did, would it still be something we'd want
to do?

00:30:32.760 --> 00:30:36.299
And its effect on the labor force, on employment?

00:30:36.299 --> 00:30:42.320
Again, stepping back just a second, one of
the real issues with speaking categorically

00:30:42.320 --> 00:30:46.480
about these things is that they are being
deployed without our knowledge.

00:30:46.480 --> 00:30:52.350
So we don't know how often we encounter a
system that classifies us, and as the classified,

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:57.350
we don't often know that the results, the
opportunities we're given or denied, the resources

00:30:57.350 --> 00:31:01.770
we're given or denied, we're actually informed
by an algorithmic backend system.

00:31:01.770 --> 00:31:03.980
There is no transparency about these systems.

00:31:03.980 --> 00:31:07.700
We don't have standardized ways to measure
them, and they're often sold by vendors to

00:31:07.700 --> 00:31:10.129
businesses without informing the public.

00:31:10.129 --> 00:31:12.529
This is a huge issue.

00:31:12.529 --> 00:31:17.309
I can talk about what we do know about their
impact, but I think we need to level set there

00:31:17.309 --> 00:31:23.100
that there is a huge need for transparency
and accountability.

00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:27.379
Aviv, do you think that people, since we've
heard about the Facebook scandal and we've

00:31:27.379 --> 00:31:32.309
heard about so many things, do you think people
do understand this threat better now?

00:31:32.309 --> 00:31:37.919
I think far better than they did, let's say,
three months ago, six months ago, a year ago,

00:31:37.919 --> 00:31:39.950
a year and a half ago.

00:31:39.950 --> 00:31:46.429
The good part of a lot of the current crisis
is at least people are more aware of the way

00:31:46.429 --> 00:31:51.200
in which technology is impacting their day-to-day,
what information they consume, how they're

00:31:51.200 --> 00:31:57.360
being manipulated in some cases, and also
the recommendation systems that affect what

00:31:57.360 --> 00:31:59.100
they see and what they believe.

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:04.649
I think just the fact that algorithm has become
a word that many people know is a new phenomenon

00:32:04.649 --> 00:32:05.980
I think over the last year.

00:32:05.980 --> 00:32:08.779
That is very valuable, because in the same
way that government that people need to know,

00:32:08.779 --> 00:32:09.779
algorithm is a word people need to know.

00:32:09.779 --> 00:32:10.980
There's another issue that you're all I think
heading towards.

00:32:10.980 --> 00:32:16.769
It's the fact that a small handful of people
who are designing a lot of these come from,

00:32:16.769 --> 00:32:24.840
well, they're white men in the Bay Area basically
As the woman of the panel, yes, they are.

00:32:24.840 --> 00:32:32.669
They're white men in the Bay Area in the Western
context, and they are of a similar upper middle-class

00:32:32.669 --> 00:32:35.039
to very rich social strata.

00:32:35.039 --> 00:32:39.490
They are often educated with exactly the same
background, because to do this, we're not

00:32:39.490 --> 00:32:44.960
just talking about going to coding camp, we're
talking about a series of very specific advanced

00:32:44.960 --> 00:32:48.200
degrees.

00:32:48.200 --> 00:32:52.909
For all the best intentions, and some of these
people might be your friends, that's a worldview.

00:32:52.909 --> 00:32:57.629
That's a worldview that is embedded in, say,
the features you choose to emphasize in your

00:32:57.629 --> 00:32:59.000
cat detection, right?

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:03.231
That's a worldview that's embedded in how
you understand gender classification, how

00:33:03.231 --> 00:33:06.309
you understand some of these softer relational
categories.

00:33:06.309 --> 00:33:12.710
That's a worldview that becomes baked into
this technology that then scales globally,

00:33:12.710 --> 00:33:20.929
seriously globally, in ways that are remapping
the world according to that vision.

00:33:20.929 --> 00:33:22.480
We should be worried about that.

00:33:22.480 --> 00:33:28.639
Indeed, the data scientist Cathy O'Neil has
terms a coin for algorithms that are secret

00:33:28.639 --> 00:33:31.740
and harmful, weapons of math destruction.

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:34.980
Yeah, it's a good pun.

00:33:34.980 --> 00:33:39.600
One thing that would help discourse about
these issues is to always remember that AI

00:33:39.600 --> 00:33:46.419
is, always will be, always should be, nothing
more than a tool designed, owned, managed,

00:33:46.419 --> 00:33:48.139
deployed by human beings.

00:33:48.139 --> 00:33:53.610
So whenever you're talking about AI and its
impact or its influence or claims, it's never

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:58.830
claims by AI, it's claims about AI made by
purveyors of AI.

00:33:58.830 --> 00:34:03.919
It's peeking behind the hood, looking who
owns it, who designs it, who manages it, and

00:34:03.919 --> 00:34:07.129
it tends to be concentrated in certain ways.

00:34:07.129 --> 00:34:08.399
Go ahead.

00:34:08.399 --> 00:34:11.110
I would just quickly add to that because I
completely agree.

00:34:11.110 --> 00:34:15.990
We also need to look at whose power it serves,
because when we're talking about the workplace

00:34:15.990 --> 00:34:19.040
examples you gave, right on, right?

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:22.030
When is the last time you went to a CVS and
the automated checkout worked?

00:34:22.030 --> 00:34:30.480
It's not replacing us anytime soon, but what
it is doing is it is increasingly monitoring,

00:34:30.480 --> 00:34:35.929
surveilling, and determining which workers
are valuable, good, and promotable, and which

00:34:35.929 --> 00:34:36.929
aren't.

00:34:36.929 --> 00:34:41.159
Of course, this is trained on data gathered
from the current workforce, which shows deep

00:34:41.159 --> 00:34:44.889
skews in inequity, and I can get into some
examples there.

00:34:44.889 --> 00:34:50.010
But this is a question of power alongside
a question of exactly how the technology is

00:34:50.010 --> 00:34:51.980
constructed.

00:34:51.980 --> 00:34:56.240
Just to add one thing beyond that, it's not
just what power it serves, but it's also what

00:34:56.240 --> 00:35:01.690
power it entrenches, as in as a result of
this technology, like being deployed in this

00:35:01.690 --> 00:35:06.050
way, who now has more power than they had
before and what can they now do with that?

00:35:06.050 --> 00:35:12.750
Are there some areas, do any of you feel,
where this new technology should never be

00:35:12.750 --> 00:35:13.750
used?

00:35:13.750 --> 00:35:17.380
Some institutions were algorithms should never
be used?

00:35:17.380 --> 00:35:22.710
I think the question is not do I want to put
my foot down and say never here ever.

00:35:22.710 --> 00:35:27.339
I think the question is what proof do we have
that they work and how do we validate that?

00:35:27.339 --> 00:35:29.730
In most cases, we have very little proof at
all.

00:35:29.730 --> 00:35:33.660
We haven't even started developing the mechanisms
to say that this is actually safe.

00:35:33.660 --> 00:35:36.609
This actually works across populations.

00:35:36.609 --> 00:35:39.680
This actually does what the marketing department
says it does.

00:35:39.680 --> 00:35:43.880
So I think the more sensitive the area we
deploy it into, say education, healthcare,

00:35:43.880 --> 00:35:48.420
et cetera, the more we have to wait until
we have those techniques in place before we

00:35:48.420 --> 00:35:49.730
trust it.

00:35:49.730 --> 00:35:54.250
That being said though, I think it is interesting
chasing after problems where even if we did

00:35:54.250 --> 00:35:58.170
all that verification and it turns out that
it is better, that for other values and other

00:35:58.170 --> 00:35:59.810
reasons we might still say no.

00:35:59.810 --> 00:36:03.920
I think those classes are a very tricky set
of classes where, I think like in the justice

00:36:03.920 --> 00:36:07.370
context specifically, where we could actually
imagine, well, maybe we actually do introduce

00:36:07.370 --> 00:36:10.710
these systems that are measurably better,
but maybe there's something about justice

00:36:10.710 --> 00:36:14.859
or providing the death sentence that actually
requires a human to be in that.

00:36:14.859 --> 00:36:17.510
So the relevant question is what's better,
right?

00:36:17.510 --> 00:36:18.510
Sure.

00:36:18.510 --> 00:36:22.170
Better in terms of accuracy with respect to
a scientifically defined or computation problem

00:36:22.170 --> 00:36:23.580
that we can all agree on?

00:36:23.580 --> 00:36:30.400
Or better in terms of some set of values that
may very well be about fairness, equity, due

00:36:30.400 --> 00:36:36.540
process, or other values that aren't necessarily
easily captured in sort of an empirical question

00:36:36.540 --> 00:36:37.540
about accuracy?

00:36:37.540 --> 00:36:41.349
Those are constantly trade-offs across a whole
host of areas.

00:36:41.349 --> 00:36:46.420
So one way to think about it is for every
given case to be made in favor of using AI

00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:50.060
as a tool in a particular context of the sort
your question raise-

00:36:50.060 --> 00:36:51.660
Like the priority of a doctor's office, for
example?

00:36:51.660 --> 00:36:56.010
Right, so you should compare alternative tools.

00:36:56.010 --> 00:37:02.020
Comparing various tools in context provides
a means for thinking about accuracy, effects,

00:37:02.020 --> 00:37:08.200
power relationships, who's behind the tools,
where's the information flow behind the scenes,

00:37:08.200 --> 00:37:11.190
or where is information coming and going from?

00:37:11.190 --> 00:37:19.119
Those are all a host of complex contextual
variables that aren't easily even captured

00:37:19.119 --> 00:37:21.640
in most of the conversations about AI.

00:37:21.640 --> 00:37:28.849
I would also add that we need to also think
about what are the checks and balances that

00:37:28.849 --> 00:37:31.020
we're putting into place as we deploy these
systems.

00:37:31.020 --> 00:37:34.780
Maybe it does make sense in this context,
even in this criminal justice context, as

00:37:34.780 --> 00:37:39.910
we consider the trade-offs and our values
around it, which is absolutely crucial.

00:37:39.910 --> 00:37:43.910
But then what are the checks to make sure
that this is being used in the way that we

00:37:43.910 --> 00:37:44.910
want it to be used?

00:37:44.910 --> 00:37:50.250
Because if we're just dealing with black box
systems, that doesn't necessarily fly in some

00:37:50.250 --> 00:37:51.250
contexts.

00:37:51.250 --> 00:37:53.020
Right, so now let's look at some more variables.

00:37:53.020 --> 00:37:58.710
We have to add into this, this recent phenomenon
of fake news, which first popped up before

00:37:58.710 --> 00:38:00.140
the 2016 election.

00:38:00.140 --> 00:38:05.400
We began seeing dubious news stories online
that were engineered for maximum disruption.

00:38:05.400 --> 00:38:09.330
What happens when anyone can make it appear
as if anything has happened, regardless of

00:38:09.330 --> 00:38:10.580
whether it did or not?

00:38:10.580 --> 00:38:14.600
Let's look at this short film we have on that
difficult question.

00:38:14.600 --> 00:38:18.250
All this information is coming from all these
different sources, and it's getting harder

00:38:18.250 --> 00:38:23.450
and harder and harder to understand and to
figure out for ourselves what is actually

00:38:23.450 --> 00:38:26.120
true and what is not.

00:38:26.120 --> 00:38:30.740
And I want you all to know that we are fighting
the fake news.

00:38:30.740 --> 00:38:38.040
It's fake, phony, fake.

00:38:38.040 --> 00:38:40.619
Fake news came out of the 2016 election.

00:38:40.619 --> 00:38:43.680
That's when it became a thing.

00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:51.630
One of the scariest advancements in AI suddenly
has been the machinery that can be used to

00:38:51.630 --> 00:38:54.290
create fake media and fake idea.

00:38:54.290 --> 00:38:59.180
Especially our friends who are lesbian, gay,
bisexual, or transgender.

00:38:59.180 --> 00:39:04.339
I visited with the families of many of the
victims on Thursday.

00:39:04.339 --> 00:39:06.980
I think the technology will get to the point
where even the media finds the expert will

00:39:06.980 --> 00:39:11.079
not be able to tell what is a true fake and
what is not.

00:39:11.079 --> 00:39:19.190
It won't be far fetched to think of it as
being used as maybe a cause for war sometimes.

00:39:19.190 --> 00:39:24.620
A country releasing a video that shows other
country is loading nuclear missiles and ready

00:39:24.620 --> 00:39:29.370
to launch, they might use that as a cause
to preemptively strike that country.

00:39:29.370 --> 00:39:35.540
Brett, you've come at this issue of fake news
from a somewhat different perspective I think.

00:39:35.540 --> 00:39:41.230
You say people often ask how can people be
so stupid to believe and engage this way,

00:39:41.230 --> 00:39:45.309
and you tell them the entire interface is
made to make you believe this way.

00:39:45.309 --> 00:39:47.190
How is it so?

00:39:47.190 --> 00:39:54.632
Right, so I got very frustrated when the story
about fake news is breaking and everyone is

00:39:54.632 --> 00:40:00.260
paying attention to it, how often I heard
the meme, I guess, that people are stupid

00:40:00.260 --> 00:40:03.140
and lazy for believing in fake news.

00:40:03.140 --> 00:40:10.290
So I would suggest that a lot of the people's
susceptibility or propensity to believe what

00:40:10.290 --> 00:40:15.780
they're seeing on various platforms is a function
of the design of the platforms.

00:40:15.780 --> 00:40:21.080
So, if you're on a social media network to
be named, you guys can figure out which one

00:40:21.080 --> 00:40:29.400
you want, if it's designed for, optimized
in its design for rapid clicking, quick scrolling,

00:40:29.400 --> 00:40:34.540
or certain forms of engagement based on profit,
where the money is to be made or where data

00:40:34.540 --> 00:40:44.220
is to be gathered, that dis-encourages or
dis-incentivizes people to stop and think

00:40:44.220 --> 00:40:50.970
or deliberate or research or ask deeper questions
about the content that they're receiving.

00:40:50.970 --> 00:41:02.250
So one species of fake news problem is a function
of making profit, creating content to get

00:41:02.250 --> 00:41:03.390
clicked, right?

00:41:03.390 --> 00:41:08.349
They could care less, those purveyors of that
kind of content could care less about convincing

00:41:08.349 --> 00:41:09.530
you or changing your beliefs.

00:41:09.530 --> 00:41:13.390
It's just click to make money and the platform
enables that.

00:41:13.390 --> 00:41:19.369
The other species is a species of content
that's all about propaganda or changing beliefs

00:41:19.369 --> 00:41:25.010
or influencing how someone approaches a problem,
and oftentimes that's not at all about making

00:41:25.010 --> 00:41:26.010
money.

00:41:26.010 --> 00:41:27.030
It's about changing beliefs.

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:35.510
To deal with that, you need some form of judgment
about the quality, the veracity, the providence

00:41:35.510 --> 00:41:39.910
of the information itself, the nature of the
content.

00:41:39.910 --> 00:41:45.330
Our current social media platforms struggle
on both fronts, both in terms of being designed

00:41:45.330 --> 00:41:53.140
for certain kinds of engagement that enable
a for-profit model, and on the other hand,

00:41:53.140 --> 00:41:59.630
for not wanting to engage in the development
of editorial judgment or the exercise of editorial

00:41:59.630 --> 00:42:04.960
judgment by human beings because that risks
putting them in sort of a content moderation

00:42:04.960 --> 00:42:06.109
position.

00:42:06.109 --> 00:42:11.760
Now that's started to change since the fake
news story has broke.

00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:16.910
Right, you've talked about this has created
a disease, techno social environment, that

00:42:16.910 --> 00:42:20.079
we're suffering from an engineered complacency.

00:42:20.079 --> 00:42:22.920
Does that frighten any of you?

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:25.099
Does it frighten you?

00:42:25.099 --> 00:42:26.930
Engineered complacency?

00:42:26.930 --> 00:42:29.349
Maybe I should explain what I mean, and then
you guys can tell me if it frightens you.

00:42:29.349 --> 00:42:31.270
It's meant to frighten, ah!

00:42:31.270 --> 00:42:37.630
No, but what I mean by engineered complacency
is that much of the environment is designed

00:42:37.630 --> 00:42:43.980
in a way that repeat interactions in the environment
lead you to feel as if it's futile to resist,

00:42:43.980 --> 00:42:46.150
there are no other options.

00:42:46.150 --> 00:42:50.680
So if you think about, the easier example
than the fake news context is maybe online

00:42:50.680 --> 00:42:51.680
contracting.

00:42:51.680 --> 00:42:55.520
So what do you do when you see an I agree
button pop up on a website?

00:42:55.520 --> 00:43:01.130
Not always, but most people most of the time
click I agree, as you're supposed to by virtue

00:43:01.130 --> 00:43:06.430
of both the design of the site and the interface,
as well as the nature of the take it or leave

00:43:06.430 --> 00:43:09.850
it interaction that you're having, the nature
of the transaction.

00:43:09.850 --> 00:43:15.579
Plus the fact that you repeat interactions
with that interface over time, so even if

00:43:15.579 --> 00:43:20.180
you thought, you know what, maybe I'll stop
and think about the legal relationship I'm

00:43:20.180 --> 00:43:24.450
entering into for some of the websites that
I'm visiting or some of the apps that I'm

00:43:24.450 --> 00:43:30.400
downloading or some of the devices I'm installing
in my home, how do you decide which of the

00:43:30.400 --> 00:43:34.130
many different encounters with the interface
would 'cause you to stop and think?

00:43:34.130 --> 00:43:35.670
Indeed, that raises another question.

00:43:35.670 --> 00:43:38.280
Are we actually changing the way the brain
operates?

00:43:38.280 --> 00:43:41.530
To what effect is this, Tim?

00:43:41.530 --> 00:43:49.200
Well, I would say I'm not a neuroscientist,
but I think there is habit formation.

00:43:49.200 --> 00:43:50.410
I think there's no doubt about that.

00:43:50.410 --> 00:43:53.809
I think one of the things that Brett's point
out, which I think is really great, is that

00:43:53.809 --> 00:43:59.820
your experience of certain elements online
train you for how you behave in future experiences

00:43:59.820 --> 00:44:00.820
with that, right?

00:44:00.820 --> 00:44:01.820
Yes.

00:44:01.820 --> 00:44:04.380
So I could even imagine a strange world that
we're in right now where we say everybody

00:44:04.380 --> 00:44:07.010
gets together and they all agree that this
situation's really bad and we should do something

00:44:07.010 --> 00:44:12.260
about it, and we actually change how the design
of the software looks.

00:44:12.260 --> 00:44:16.210
But actually people's behaviors are actually
stuck in a format that will make those behaviors

00:44:16.210 --> 00:44:17.290
sticky over time.

00:44:17.290 --> 00:44:21.210
So you could actually imagine let's just bust
up and shut down Facebook tomorrow, whether

00:44:21.210 --> 00:44:25.780
or not it actually recreates itself as a result
of the fact that the market now is entrained

00:44:25.780 --> 00:44:27.450
in certain ways.

00:44:27.450 --> 00:44:29.670
I think that's an interesting possibility
and a really challenging one if you think

00:44:29.670 --> 00:44:33.040
about the fact that these two variables are
actually related, right?

00:44:33.040 --> 00:44:36.900
That people are influenced, but then they
actually influence the production in the system

00:44:36.900 --> 00:44:37.900
in return.

00:44:37.900 --> 00:44:39.339
Aviv, you're nodding vigorously there.

00:44:39.339 --> 00:44:44.660
I was just agreeing that I think this is another
example of the entrenchment of power, where

00:44:44.660 --> 00:44:50.770
here it's not even power in a particular organization,
but it's power in a particular almost process

00:44:50.770 --> 00:44:55.220
or way of using yourself and your brain and
your environment.

00:44:55.220 --> 00:45:02.710
I would add I agree and I would say and yet
it is power in a particular organization.

00:45:02.710 --> 00:45:10.210
I have never known a product manager or engineer
to get promoted for reducing engagement.

00:45:10.210 --> 00:45:16.400
There are certain types of incentive structures
that are driving the culture and ecosystems

00:45:16.400 --> 00:45:21.049
of these companies, and even if you're engaging
with propaganda that is not there to make

00:45:21.049 --> 00:45:26.230
money for clicks, the longer you stay on a
site, the more adrenalizing content you're

00:45:26.230 --> 00:45:28.599
exposed to, the more ads they show you.

00:45:28.599 --> 00:45:35.990
So there is a monetary incentive in these
ad business platforms to keep people on the

00:45:35.990 --> 00:45:40.950
site, which I think the politics of that are
becoming clear, the risks of that are becoming

00:45:40.950 --> 00:45:44.309
clear, but the business model remains pretty
scolaric.

00:45:44.309 --> 00:45:45.849
And Brett, go ahead.

00:45:45.849 --> 00:45:51.160
I'd love to add one wrinkle to this discussion
though, too, 'cause I think a lot about this

00:45:51.160 --> 00:45:55.780
Catch 22 that platforms find themselves in,
because they can say, "Okay, we're not going

00:45:55.780 --> 00:45:58.940
to take action," in which case the public
is like, "You're negligent, you didn't take

00:45:58.940 --> 00:45:59.940
action."

00:45:59.940 --> 00:46:01.150
Then they're like, "Okay, okay, so we're going
to take action."

00:46:01.150 --> 00:46:03.240
Then they're like, "You're imposing your ethical
will on me."

00:46:03.240 --> 00:46:06.829
I think this actually is part of the tricky
thing that needs to be dealt with because

00:46:06.829 --> 00:46:10.530
I think when people ask the question, "Facebook,
do you have a responsibility here?"

00:46:10.530 --> 00:46:14.859
the question is should they be the ones who
are delegated that responsibility?

00:46:14.859 --> 00:46:19.480
That ends up being a very interesting and
tricky question, and I worry about situations

00:46:19.480 --> 00:46:24.380
where we say they could just do better, 'cause
in some ways it sort of reinforces their position

00:46:24.380 --> 00:46:26.329
in making a lot of these decisions as well.

00:46:26.329 --> 00:46:29.960
So we may need to actually even rethink the
governance of how these things were designed

00:46:29.960 --> 00:46:31.650
in a much bigger way.

00:46:31.650 --> 00:46:36.869
I would caution against us/them mentality
in general.

00:46:36.869 --> 00:46:42.160
I think oftentimes, but it varies by context
and it varies by the technology we're talking

00:46:42.160 --> 00:46:43.160
about.

00:46:43.160 --> 00:46:48.640
The rest of you must have strong feelings
about this.

00:46:48.640 --> 00:46:54.050
I'm concerned about what I call the Overton
window of crisis here.

00:46:54.050 --> 00:46:56.900
The over-
The Overton window of crisis.

00:46:56.900 --> 00:47:00.660
Overton window, it's a concept from political
science that talks about what's politically

00:47:00.660 --> 00:47:02.359
acceptable?

00:47:02.359 --> 00:47:06.420
The notion is that certain events occasionally
will happen that will widen that window, where

00:47:06.420 --> 00:47:11.260
someone breaks various norms in politics and
suddenly a whole lot of activity is allowed

00:47:11.260 --> 00:47:15.230
that wasn't allowed before, it becomes plausible
in a way that it wasn't.

00:47:15.230 --> 00:47:19.510
I think there was a view early on around these
privacy debates which is, okay, people don't

00:47:19.510 --> 00:47:22.859
care about privacy right now, but we just
need to wait until the first big crisis, the

00:47:22.859 --> 00:47:25.940
first big data bridge, and then everybody
will care about privacy.

00:47:25.940 --> 00:47:29.854
Then that moment came and went, and then came
and went, and came and went, and came and

00:47:29.854 --> 00:47:33.880
went, and you get this feeling now that actually
that state of affairs has been normalized

00:47:33.880 --> 00:47:39.319
in a way that actually makes it quite difficult
to mobilize change politically, within the

00:47:39.319 --> 00:47:40.319
ministry.

00:47:40.319 --> 00:47:43.319
I was talking to a person who does product
management recently.

00:47:43.319 --> 00:47:45.810
I was like, "Oh, well, you know this product
you're building, you can kind of improve it

00:47:45.810 --> 00:47:47.369
in X, Y, Z ways."

00:47:47.369 --> 00:47:51.250
He said, "Well, you know, we're going to get
blamed by the public enemies no matter what

00:47:51.250 --> 00:47:53.160
we do, so we're just going to do it."

00:47:53.160 --> 00:47:58.800
I think that's a really interesting phenomenon
where basically the response has been so built

00:47:58.800 --> 00:48:03.819
into the process that that creates a level
of apathy onto itself.

00:48:03.819 --> 00:48:09.830
And the effectiveness of dissent has been
muted so much that that's just part of it.

00:48:09.830 --> 00:48:13.160
You look at the media for a while.

00:48:13.160 --> 00:48:18.030
There may be some stories about it, but there
is not effective organizing.

00:48:18.030 --> 00:48:25.220
I guess another thing about this is I find
the current framework of privacy broken to

00:48:25.220 --> 00:48:28.410
talk about a lot of what we're speaking about.

00:48:28.410 --> 00:48:34.450
I don't know that I've ever personally felt
or detected harm from a privacy breach, although

00:48:34.450 --> 00:48:40.799
I've been in all sorts of databases that have
leaked because of egregious security practices.

00:48:40.799 --> 00:48:45.910
But I do know that we have collective harms,
and I think we need to begin thinking about

00:48:45.910 --> 00:48:48.420
these things collectively.

00:48:48.420 --> 00:48:55.410
It's not I keep my personal data safe, it's
that we have major interests, the Federal

00:48:55.410 --> 00:49:00.970
Government and this smartwatch or whatever
it is, large tech companies who are interested

00:49:00.970 --> 00:49:06.170
in understanding this data and then creating
profiles of us based on the data.

00:49:06.170 --> 00:49:10.369
So I may not have contributed my data, but
I am suddenly profiled because there is a

00:49:10.369 --> 00:49:13.220
model about childhood activity.

00:49:13.220 --> 00:49:15.039
Who knows where that's going to go?

00:49:15.039 --> 00:49:18.480
Is that going to be used to weight college
admissions?

00:49:18.480 --> 00:49:22.589
Will that be sent to insurance companies to
see whose family gets insurance?

00:49:22.589 --> 00:49:28.640
But we have not asked for transparency or
oversight into these kinds of processes and

00:49:28.640 --> 00:49:29.640
assumptions.

00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:33.030
So when we talk about privacy, it's often
sort of a very thin debate, 'cause I don't

00:49:33.030 --> 00:49:36.780
have time to do my laundry let alone read
through a TOS and then take responsibility

00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:39.010
for where all of my data is.

00:49:39.010 --> 00:49:40.010
Aviv.

00:49:40.010 --> 00:49:43.099
I think that one of the other things to really
keep in mind here is that there are really

00:49:43.099 --> 00:49:46.660
hard trade-offs being made when you're thinking
about privacy.

00:49:46.660 --> 00:49:52.130
So one example is if you were talking about
misinformation in the 2016 election or even

00:49:52.130 --> 00:49:59.140
stuff that's being spread in let's say Myanmar,
where you might have people creating fake

00:49:59.140 --> 00:50:01.650
accounts, and those fake accounts are spreading
this propaganda.

00:50:01.650 --> 00:50:05.500
That propaganda is causing violence or it's
misinforming voters.

00:50:05.500 --> 00:50:08.599
Well, how does a Facebook go and actually
detect those fake accounts?

00:50:08.599 --> 00:50:11.960
Well, they're looking at the IPs of all these
users.

00:50:11.960 --> 00:50:16.090
They're looking at every single signal they
can possibly get on that user and comparing

00:50:16.090 --> 00:50:20.420
it to what they know are real users and to
tell the difference.

00:50:20.420 --> 00:50:24.840
So if they don't have that data, they can't
make that comparison, then that makes their

00:50:24.840 --> 00:50:26.599
job a lot harder in protecting our democracy.

00:50:26.599 --> 00:50:30.470
In fact, they're trying to do that which they
now ostensibly are.

00:50:30.470 --> 00:50:35.109
So I think that there are some very, very
difficult balances that we need to strike

00:50:35.109 --> 00:50:39.820
in order to ensure that we protect our democracy,
we protect our privacy, we protect our ability

00:50:39.820 --> 00:50:41.000
to speak freely.

00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:44.400
All of these things are in the balance and
there's a lot of values that are in tension.

00:50:44.400 --> 00:50:50.710
So you've set up the big question then, we
are now going to I guess move to the inevitable,

00:50:50.710 --> 00:50:54.710
hopeful side of this, if we can find one.

00:50:54.710 --> 00:50:55.710
What are the fixes?

00:50:55.710 --> 00:50:56.710
What are we to do?

00:50:56.710 --> 00:51:00.610
Tim, how can we make this AI more ethical?

00:51:00.610 --> 00:51:06.950
Sure, so one of the things I think about particularly
related to this discussion is the delegation

00:51:06.950 --> 00:51:10.930
question that I mentioned a little while back,
which is that particularly after 2016 we're

00:51:10.930 --> 00:51:14.400
kind of in this tough spot where we're basically
like companies just solve the problem.

00:51:14.400 --> 00:51:17.210
Well, we feel really uncomfortable about Facebook
telling us what's true or not.

00:51:17.210 --> 00:51:18.640
All right, well, then let's pass a law.

00:51:18.640 --> 00:51:21.065
Then you're like, "Oh, we don't actually want
government to tell us what's true or not."

00:51:21.065 --> 00:51:25.950
So there's actually this moment of we have
to delegate to these two wolves, where both

00:51:25.950 --> 00:51:30.730
of them we feel actually pretty uncomfortable
working with them on issues of truth, for

00:51:30.730 --> 00:51:31.730
instance.

00:51:31.730 --> 00:51:38.900
It seems, particularly after 2016, there's
a general implosion I think in our faith around

00:51:38.900 --> 00:51:45.320
the ability for communities to self-organize
and deal with some of these problems themselves.

00:51:45.320 --> 00:51:49.130
I think there is good intellectual work to
be done in terms of trying to figure out how

00:51:49.130 --> 00:51:52.780
platforms can make those things more robust.

00:51:52.780 --> 00:51:53.780
Right.

00:51:53.780 --> 00:51:57.510
Aviv, I see you nodding a great deal.

00:51:57.510 --> 00:52:00.790
We're hearing also every week from the platforms
that are stepping up.

00:52:00.790 --> 00:52:05.309
Twitter says it's going to limit tweets from
people behaving badly, and Facebook has just

00:52:05.309 --> 00:52:08.350
detailed policing for sex, terror, and hate
content.

00:52:08.350 --> 00:52:11.619
Do you think they're going far enough?

00:52:11.619 --> 00:52:14.214
I think there's a number of steps that are
in the right direction, and there's going

00:52:14.214 --> 00:52:18.590
to be some steps that backtrack as we learn
more and as we discover that, oh, wait, this

00:52:18.590 --> 00:52:19.720
isn't exactly what we wanted.

00:52:19.720 --> 00:52:21.890
I think that's part of this process.

00:52:21.890 --> 00:52:26.480
I'm happy that we're actually on this process
as opposed to just ignoring it, which was

00:52:26.480 --> 00:52:32.910
sort of the status quo two years ago when
they said fake news is a 2016 problem that's

00:52:32.910 --> 00:52:34.140
in the US.

00:52:34.140 --> 00:52:39.060
A lot of the rest of the world has been dealing
with misinformation that's very deeply affecting

00:52:39.060 --> 00:52:43.380
their political systems for five, ten years
even.

00:52:43.380 --> 00:52:48.180
I think that there are some things that really
do need to happen, and they're starting to

00:52:48.180 --> 00:52:50.609
happen within these organizations.

00:52:50.609 --> 00:53:00.010
In particular, we need to have empowered teams
that can look at the impacts of the technologies

00:53:00.010 --> 00:53:05.609
on these things that we care about, on let's
say our democracy, on inter-community trust

00:53:05.609 --> 00:53:07.980
so that you're not just increasing polarization.

00:53:07.980 --> 00:53:12.450
If you don't have someone who's paid to ensure
that your platform isn't deeply polarizing

00:53:12.450 --> 00:53:15.710
things, well, then your growth team that's
trying to get more users and trying to get

00:53:15.710 --> 00:53:18.230
more action might unintentionally just do
that.

00:53:18.230 --> 00:53:21.740
There has to be that back and forth, and they
have to be empowered enough to push back.

00:53:21.740 --> 00:53:27.800
Just fascinating possible solution to some
of it, empowered by whom?

00:53:27.800 --> 00:53:32.630
Well, both within the organization and ideally
with some sort of external accountability

00:53:32.630 --> 00:53:35.230
where across these organizations.

00:53:35.230 --> 00:53:39.890
There's a number of different structures,
but the idea is that you not only have some

00:53:39.890 --> 00:53:45.430
sort of internal structure that's keeping
an eye on this with people who their job responsibility

00:53:45.430 --> 00:53:51.380
is to maintain some of these things that we
hear about, but you also have outside of the

00:53:51.380 --> 00:53:55.069
company, some way in which you're able to
see, oh, is that company doing a good job

00:53:55.069 --> 00:53:56.329
with this?

00:53:56.329 --> 00:54:01.890
Whether that's through a regulatory process,
maybe, maybe not, but I think there has to

00:54:01.890 --> 00:54:04.550
be some structure that creates those power
dynamics.

00:54:04.550 --> 00:54:10.140
So an independent idealistic new profession
that we need to live in this new world?

00:54:10.140 --> 00:54:12.570
I mean, I don't know that it's new.

00:54:12.570 --> 00:54:16.400
Social scientists have been doing this sort
of work for a million years.

00:54:16.400 --> 00:54:19.520
Maybe not a million, don't quote me on that.

00:54:19.520 --> 00:54:22.160
Maybe, I don't know.

00:54:22.160 --> 00:54:24.809
It feels like it.

00:54:24.809 --> 00:54:33.180
But I think the combination of people looking
at how the technology is impacting society

00:54:33.180 --> 00:54:38.839
and really cross-functionally and having teams
that are doing that and that is their focus,

00:54:38.839 --> 00:54:41.410
to understand the way these platforms are
impacting things.

00:54:41.410 --> 00:54:46.579
I think even beyond that when you're talking
about something like these deep fakes, that's

00:54:46.579 --> 00:54:53.760
about not just stuff that's happening in these
companies, in these platforms, but it's happening

00:54:53.760 --> 00:54:58.039
in research labs where you have researchers
who are just like, "Oh, wouldn't this be cool?

00:54:58.039 --> 00:54:59.779
Oh, I can get a good paper out of this."

00:54:59.779 --> 00:55:05.569
That's great, I love good papers, I love cool
things.

00:55:05.569 --> 00:55:09.930
But it doesn't mean that you shouldn't be
looking at what are the potential negative

00:55:09.930 --> 00:55:15.200
impacts, and one thing that I've been really
excited to see is people within the academic

00:55:15.200 --> 00:55:17.220
community starting to really think about this
deeply.

00:55:17.220 --> 00:55:25.260
There's actually as of a few days ago, if
you go to negative impacts.org, there's this

00:55:25.260 --> 00:55:36.670
group, the ACM Computing of the Future, something
along those lines, where they're looking at

00:55:36.670 --> 00:55:40.170
the ways in which we can improve the purity
of process to really incorporate not just

00:55:40.170 --> 00:55:43.690
the positive impacts of a technology, but
also keeping in mind the negative impacts.

00:55:43.690 --> 00:55:48.640
All right, we have just a couple of minutes
left, so your thoughts about where we're headed

00:55:48.640 --> 00:55:54.460
if we don't get ...
We're clearly headed to climate crisis, we're

00:55:54.460 --> 00:55:58.890
clearly headed to a lot of really serious
issues that relate to this and don't relate

00:55:58.890 --> 00:55:59.890
to this.

00:55:59.890 --> 00:56:04.490
But I want to backup a little and make space
for the question, what world do we actually

00:56:04.490 --> 00:56:06.030
want?

00:56:06.030 --> 00:56:13.339
Do we want Facebook to be negotiating with
the US government to determine truthiness,

00:56:13.339 --> 00:56:16.380
or do we want Facebook?

00:56:16.380 --> 00:56:18.710
Do we want capitalism?

00:56:18.710 --> 00:56:20.789
What do we want this to be?

00:56:20.789 --> 00:56:26.569
Then think about what types of technologies
could you build or avoid building to create

00:56:26.569 --> 00:56:27.569
that?

00:56:27.569 --> 00:56:31.849
Because I think we fall into the trap of accepting
the status quo and then trying to tweak it

00:56:31.849 --> 00:56:32.940
around the edges.

00:56:32.940 --> 00:56:38.460
It's very clear that that's not at this point
working and that a number of the major social

00:56:38.460 --> 00:56:43.431
problems we're facing are enmeshed deeply
with the major technological issues and they

00:56:43.431 --> 00:56:49.420
are being exacerbated in ways that are very
complex to tease apart from a systems perspective.

00:56:49.420 --> 00:56:54.990
So I would want to start with the imaginative
potential of how do we create a better world?

00:56:54.990 --> 00:57:00.510
And then see do we want these bulwarks of
power in this world in that world we have

00:57:00.510 --> 00:57:01.510
yet to create?

00:57:01.510 --> 00:57:02.510
Okay, and the last word, sorry to interrupt,
but we have just one.

00:57:02.510 --> 00:57:03.779
Where are we headed if we don't fix it?

00:57:03.779 --> 00:57:12.740
I think we're heading to science fiction dystopia
where more and more of our lives are predicted

00:57:12.740 --> 00:57:17.400
and determined by technological systems controlled
by a few.

00:57:17.400 --> 00:57:19.809
How we avoid that?

00:57:19.809 --> 00:57:24.369
The constitutional, the most fundamental issue
for us to confront in the 21st century is

00:57:24.369 --> 00:57:29.140
how to sustain our freedom to be off so that
we're not always on.

00:57:29.140 --> 00:57:34.640
How to build techno social environments through
technology and social institutions that preserve

00:57:34.640 --> 00:57:40.420
under determinate environments within which
we can develop, play, experiment, interact

00:57:40.420 --> 00:57:44.299
with each other, in ways that allow us to
develop our own lives rather than have them

00:57:44.299 --> 00:57:45.460
determined for us.

00:57:45.460 --> 00:57:49.170
It sounds like something for the lawyers,
the politicians, and everybody to get involved

00:57:49.170 --> 00:57:52.890
with, including you nerds who have given us
all this stuff.

00:57:52.890 --> 00:57:56.279
Let's give it a round of applause.

00:57:56.279 --> 00:58:03.250
Thank you for sharing.

